  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-10-07T06:23:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "dc55d41a-dcce-4e25-abf8-67ff133c2fc7",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 070623\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1122 PM PDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Well above-normal temperatures will persist through Friday, with \n  dangerous heat expected in Death Valley.\n\n* Chances for gusty winds, cooler temperatures, and potentially\n  widespread precipitation arrive Sunday through Tuesday. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through early next week.\n\nWeak shortwave that brought a few showers and thunderstorms to \ncentral Nevada Tuesday will continue to shift east today as high \npressure strengthen over the region. There will still be enough mid-\nlevel moisture over the area to bring a few afternoon clouds, but no \nprecipitation is expected. Temperatures will peak this afternoon \nthrough Friday with readings generally 10-15 degrees above normal. \nMajor to extreme heat risk will persist in Death Valley as daytime \nhigh temperatures climb into the 100s each day, and may approach 110 \ndegrees during peak heating. \n\nSome big changes could be heading our way this weekend and early \nnext week. First, a deep upper level trough is forecast to dig \nsouthward out of the Pacific Northwest, and interact with a weak \nupper low off of the southern California. This weak low is expected \nto absorb remnant moisture from Hurricane Rachel and eventually get \npulled northeast into southern California and Arizona Sunday as the \ntrough continues to dig. The models are indicating fairly \nsignificant moisture getting pulled into the area with PWs forecast \nto be around 200-250 percent of normal for this time of year. There \nhas been very little overall change with the timing and track, but \nthe latest model runs have trended a little further west which could \nput areas like eastern San Bernardino, Clark, Lincoln, and Mohave \ncounties with the potential of seeing widespread areas of heavy rain \nSunday afternoon through Monday. Precipitation chances look to hang \non at least through mid-week as a closed low develops over central \nNevada and is slow to exit. Although the models remain in good \nagreement, we could still see changes the the depth of the trough \nand the track of Hurricane Rachel which would alter the overall \nforecast. Outside of the precipitation chances, temperatures \ntrending below normal along with gusty conditions can also be \nexpected. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light\nwinds will generally follow typical daily directional trends with\noccasional variability through Wednesday evening. VFR conditions \nwill prevail with FEW mid level clouds developing in the \nafternoon. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds will generally \nfollow typical daily directional trends with sustained speeds \naround 5 to 10 knots through the TAF period. The exception will be\ndown the Colorado River Valley, including KIFP, where 10 to 15 \nknot northerly winds are expected on Wednesday afternoon. VFR \nconditions will prevail with FEW mid-level clouds in the \nafternoon.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           WED, OCT 7  THU, OCT 8  FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     99(1978)*   98(2024)*   97(2024)*\nBishop        94(1965)*   95(1996)    93(2015)\nNeedles       107(2024)   107(2024)   104(1996)*\nDaggett       102(2024)   101(1996)*  103(1980)\nKingman       99(2024)    98(1996)    98(1910)\nDesert Rock   99(2024)    98(1996)*   97(1996)\nDeath Valley  109(2024)*  112(1996)*  112(1996)\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      WED, OCT 7  THU, OCT 8  FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     75(2024)*   76(2024)*   72(2024)*\nBishop        57(1943)    51(1960)    61(2025)\nNeedles       76(1947)*   76(2024)*   76(1988)*\nDaggett       72(2024)*   69(1980)*   69(1996)*\nKingman       69(1947)*   67(1960)*   68(1926)*\nDesert Rock   65(1980)*   70(2024)    65(1991)*\nDeath Valley  80(1943)*   85(1964)*   78(2024)*\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Gorelow\nAVIATION...Stessman\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
